"Can maiBERT Speak for Maithili? (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.15048v1)\nciteturn29799view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.15048v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 297\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Dataset L18:     1. cite8†2.1 Pre-training Corpus: L19:   4. cite9†3 Related Works L20:   5. cite10†4 Methodology L21:   6. cite11†5 Experimental Setup L22:     1. cite12†5.1 Evaluation Metrics L23:     2. cite13†5.2 Baselines L24:   7. cite14†6 Results and Discussion L25:     1. cite15†6.1 LLM Pre-training L26:     2. cite16†6.2 Downstream Classification and Comparison with SOTA L27:     3. cite17†6.3 Computational Experiments L28:     4. cite18†6.4 Traditional Machine Learning Baselines L29:   8. cite19†7 Conclusion and Future Works L30:   9. cite20†References L31: cite21†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L32: \nL33: arXiv:2509.15048v1 [cs.CL] 18 Sep 2025\nL34: # Can maiBERT Speak for Maithili?\nL35: Sumit Yadav Affiliation: IOE, Pulchowk Campus, Lalitpur, Nepal Email: 076bct088.sumit@pcampus.edu.np    Raju Kumar Yadav Affiliation: IOE, Pulchowk Campus, Lalitpur, Nepal Email: 076bct100.raju@pcampus.edu.np    Utsav Maskey Email: ganesh@pcampus.edu.np Affiliation: Macquarie University, Sydney, Australia    Gautam Siddharth Kashyap Affiliation: Macquarie University, Sydney, Australia Email: utsav.maskey@mq.edu.au    Md Azizul Hoque Affiliation: Macquarie University, Sydney, Australia Email: gautam.kashyap@hdr.mq.edu.au    Ganesh Gautam Affiliation: IOE, Pulchowk Campus, Lalitpur, Nepal Email: mdazizul.hoque@students.mq.edu.au\nL36: ###### Abstract\nL37: Natural Language Understanding (NLU) for low-resource languages remains a major challenge in NLP due to the scarcity of high-quality data and language-specific models. Maithili, despite being spoken by millions, lacks adequate computational resources, limiting its inclusion in digital and AI-driven applications. To address this gap, we introduce maiBERT, a BERT-based language model pre-trained specifically for Maithili using the Masked Language Modeling (MLM) technique.\nL38: Our model is trained on a newly constructed Maithili corpus and evaluated through a news classification task. In our experiments, maiBERT achieved an accuracy of 87.02%, outperforming existing regional models like NepBERTa and HindiBERT, with a 0.13% overall accuracy gain and 5–7% improvement across various classes.\nL39: We have open-sourced maiBERT on Hugging Face^{1}^{1} 1 cite22†https://huggingface.co/rockerritesh/maiBERT˙TF†huggingface.co , enabling further fine-tuning for downstream tasks such as sentiment analysis and Named Entity Recognition (NER).\nL40: ## 1 Introduction\nL41: Natural Language Processing (NLP) has seen rapid advancements in recent years, with the development of sophisticated models like BERT (cite23†Devlin et al., 2019 ), GPT (cite24†Radford et al., 2018 ), and T5 (cite25†Raffel et al., 2020 ) that enable machines to understand, interpret, and generate human language. However, most of this progress has primarily focused on high-resource languages such as English, Mandarin, and Hindi.\nL42: Low-resource languages—languages that lack sufficient digital content, annotated corpora, and computational resources—have remained largely underserved. One such language is Maithili^{2}^{2} 2 In terms of linguistic structure, Maithili follows a syntax and morphology that are comparable to other Devanagari-script languages such as Hindi and Nepali. It comprises 47 segmental phonemes: 8 vowel phonemes characterized by duration and quality, 39 consonant phonemes, and 10 numerals represented as letters.\nL43: as shown in Fig. cite26†1 , an Indo-Aryan language spoken by millions of people in Nepal and India, particularly in the Terai region of Nepal (mainly Madhesh Province and parts of Koshi Province) and neighboring districts in the Indian state of Bihar.\nL44: cite27†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Density-Based Map of Maithili Language\nL45: Maithili is constitutionally recognized in Nepal and India and is widely spoken in both formal and informal contexts. Despite its rich linguistic and cultural heritage, Maithili has received minimal attention in the computational linguistics community (cite28†Mundotiya et al., 2021 ).\nL46: Unlike related languages such as Hindi and Nepali, which have been the subject of substantial research (cite29†Joshi, 2022 ; cite30†Pudasaini et al., 2023 ; cite31†Maskey et al., 2022 ) and have several publicly available resources (cite32†Bal et al., 2024 ; cite33†Bafna and Žabokrtskỳ, 2022 ), Maithili lacks a comprehensive digital footprint.\nL47: There are no large-scale pre-trained language models, standardized datasets, or open-source tools dedicated to this language, which significantly impedes the development of NLP applications for Maithili speakers.\nL48: While existing models developed for related languages such as (cite34†Hartmann et al., 2017 ; cite35†Alian and Awajan, 2020 ; cite36†Pandit et al., 2019 ; cite37†Patil and Kolhe, 2022 ; cite38†Nie et al., 2022 ; cite39†Ramchandra et al., 2019 ; cite40†Lalrempuii et al., 2021 ; cite41†Priyadarshi and Saha, 2020 ; cite42†Mundotiya et al., 2022 ) have shown some promise, they fall short when applied directly to Maithili due to dialectal variations, unique lexical items, and morphological structures that distinguish it from other Indo-Aryan languages.\nL49: To bridge this critical gap, we propose a novel approach that focuses on the development of a Maithili-specific language model using the BERT architecture. Our work begins with the construction of a high-quality Maithili corpus collected from diverse sources, including online articles, books, and conversational data. This raw corpus forms the basis for pre-training a Masked Language Model (MLM) tailored specifically for Maithili.\nL50: Following pre-training, we fine-tune the model for a downstream NLP task—text classification. Our model, referred to as maiBERT, is then benchmarked against State-of-the-Art (SOTA) to evaluate performance. Moreover, to support the wider research and developer community, we have open-sourced the pre-trained maiBERT model on Hugging Face.\nL51: ## 2 Dataset\nL52: Category  | Count  | Avg Words  | Avg Sent\nL53: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL54: Culture  | 2,575  | 147.4  | 8.5\nL55: Economy  | 549  | 176.8  | 8.5\nL56: EduTech  | 619  | 57.0  | 6.7\nL57: Entertainment  | 844  | 156.1  | 8.1\nL58: Health  | 506  | 58.5  | 4.3\nL59: Interview  | 424  | 95.4  | 6.8\nL60: Literature  | 2,212  | 155.0  | 10.3\nL61: Opinion  | 805  | 123.1  | 9.9\nL62: Politics  | 2,075  | 52.1  | 4.8\nL63: Sports  | 1,774  | 135.7  | 8.1\nL64: Total  | 12,383  | 130.7  | 7.6\nL65: Table 1: Dataset overview: Distribution and statistics across text categories for Count, Average number of words and Sentences.\nL66: ### 2.1 Pre-training Corpus:\nL67: \nL68: The raw corpus^{3}^{3} 3 cite43†https://huggingface.co/datasets/rockerritesh/maithiliNewsData†huggingface.co for MLM pre-training consists of 1,028,017 sentences, with an average sentence length of 16.88 words and an average word length of 4.44 characters. Figure cite44†2 provides an overview of the Maithili text corpus, illustrating how characters, words, and key linguistic features are distributed across the dataset.\nL69: cite45†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Comprehensive text statistics of the Maithili corpus showing character distribution, word counts, and linguistic features.\nL70: \nL71: The corpus has a rich vocabulary of 1,357,081 unique tokens, highlighting its lexical diversity. A linguistic analysis revealed frequent occurrences of the Maithili-specific punctuation marker, with a total of 1,044,037 appearances across all text files—providing insights into syntactic structure and sentence boundaries.\nL72: Figure cite46†3 displays the overall word distribution patterns in the Maithili text corpus.\nL73: \nL74: cite47†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Word distribution patterns in Maithili corpus.\nL75: For enhanced language modeling, the raw data underwent extensive preprocessing, including normalization, lemmatization, and tokenization using a customized BertTokenizer^{4}^{4} 4 cite48†https://www.tensorflow.org/text/api˙docs/python/text/BertTokenizer†www.tensorflow.org , which preserved linguistic features specific to Devanagari script while filtering out non-Devanagari characters.\nL76: Classification Dataset: For downstream evaluation, we constructed a separate labeled dataset from multiple credible Maithili news sources, such as Esamaad^{5}^{5} 5 cite49†https://esamaad.com/†esamaad.com , Maithilijindabad^{6}^{6} 6 cite50†https://maithilijindabaad.com/†maithilijindabaad.com , and Maithili Purnajagran Prakash^{7}^{7} 7 cite51†https://mppdainik.com/†mppdainik.com .\nL77: \nL78: cite52†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: Distribution of news categories in the Maithili classification dataset.\nL79: The annotation process involved 3 native speakers and 2 linguistic experts who labeled the corpus into ten predefined categories, totaling 11,959 labeled instances, as shown in Figure cite53†4 . This distribution reflects the natural occurrence of topics in Maithili news media, with cultural and literary content being predominant.\nL80: \nL81: To illustrate the varying levels of content complexity across categories, Figure cite54†5 presents the distribution of word counts for different news sections.\nL82: cite55†Image: Refer to caption Figure 5: Word count distribution across different news categories in the classification dataset.\nL83: \nL84: Figure cite56†6 shows the distribution of text lengths across the classification dataset, illustrating the variety in article lengths.\nL85: \nL86: cite57†Image: Refer to caption Figure 6: Distribution of text lengths in the Maithili classification dataset.\nL87: To validate the quality and consistency of annotations, inter-annotator agreement was computed using Cohen’s Kappa coefficient, yielding a strong agreement score of 0.82, indicating reliable annotation practices. The labeled dataset was then split into training (70%), validation (15%), and test (15%) subsets to ensure robust performance evaluation. Table cite58†1 provides a comprehensive overview of the classification dataset characteristics across different text complexity levels.\nL88: ## 3 Related Works\nL89: NLP has shown remarkable advancements in foundational tasks such as semantic similarity and text categorization, significantly benefiting high-resource languages but posing persistent challenges for low-resource languages like Maithili (cite59†Chandrasekaran and Mago, 2021 ; cite60†Zhang et al., 2015 ).\nL90: While semantic similarity tasks often depend on pretrained models or lexical resources, distributional semantic models such as Latent Semantic Analysis (LSA) (cite61†Landauer et al., 1998 ), Word2Vec (cite62†Church, 2017 ), and GloVe (cite63†Schnabel et al., 2015 ) have proven effective in capturing word relationships through co-occurrence patterns. However, these models often suffer from a lack of interpretability and are limited by the quality and domain-specificity of the training corpus.\nL91: Therefore, cite64†Hadj Taieb et al. (2015) proposed a feature-based measure of sentence semantic similarity using noun, verb, and common word similarities on the Microsoft Research Paraphrase Corpus^{8}^{8} 8 cite65†https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/download/details.aspx?id=52398†www.microsoft.com (MRPC), achieving a 79% agreement with human perception; however, their approach is heavily dependent on handcrafted linguistic features, limiting generalizability across domains and languages. cite66†Colla et al.\nL92: (2020) introduced two novel similarity metrics and sense-level embeddings using six different methods, showing improved granularity in meaning representation; nonetheless, their method suffers from high computational complexity and lacks adaptation to low-resource languages. cite67†Nguyen et al.\nL93: (2019) proposed short-text semantic similarity models through pretrained embeddings and external knowledge sources, evaluating them on MRPC, STS2015^{9}^{9} 9 cite68†https://github.com/brmson/dataset-sts†github.com , and P4PIN (cite69†Álvarez-Carmona et al., 2018 ) with promising Pearson correlation scores of 84.17%, 83.73%, and 95.22%, respectively; still, the study does not investigate robustness across morphologically rich or low-resource languages. cite34†Hartmann et al.\nL94: (2017) trained 31 word embedding models for Portuguese-Brazilian using Word2Vec, FastText^{10}^{10} 10 cite70†https://huggingface.co/blog/fasttext†huggingface.co , GloVe, and Wang2Vec^{11}^{11} 11 cite71†https://github.com/wlin12/wang2vec†github.com and recommended task-specific evaluations for syntactic and semantic similarity; however, the findings are limited to Portuguese and cannot be directly extrapolated to under-resourced Indic languages.\nL95: cite35†Alian and Awajan (2020) reviewed semantic similarity techniques in Arabic and English and concluded that feature-based models outperform others; nonetheless, their study lacks empirical validation on cross-lingual or low-resource language scenarios. cite36†Pandit et al. (2019) proposed path-based and distributional models for cross-lingual semantic similarity, identifying Word2Vec as superior; yet, their evaluation lacks coverage for languages like Maithili.\nL96: With the advent of deep learning architectures like Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), text categorization tasks have also significantly improved (cite72†D’silva and Sharma, 2022 ), though early studies did not focus on low-resource settings or multilingual adaptability.\nL97: Therefore, to address the data scarcity in low-resource languages, transfer learning has emerged as a widely adopted strategy where pretrained models from high-resource languages are fine-tuned on target languages (cite73†Ali et al., 2022 ). cite37†Patil and Kolhe (2022) demonstrated this by adapting an English sentiment model for Marathi through parameter optimization, although their study lacks scalability for languages with significantly different syntax and morphology. Similarly, cite38†Nie et al.\nL98: (2022) proposed a novel cross-lingual emotion categorization model for Swahili and Urdu, but the absence of domain-specific datasets restricts real-world applicability. cite39†Ramchandra et al. (2019) explored semantic similarity in Hindi using multilingual deep learning models such as BERT and LASER, highlighting their comparative effectiveness; however, their results do not consider dialectal or regional variations commonly found in Hindi’s linguistic relatives. cite40†Lalrempuii et al.\nL99: (2021) developed a NER framework for Assamese using manually annotated data and neural networks, achieving reasonable accuracy; however, the manual annotation process is labor-intensive and limits scalability. cite41†Priyadarshi and Saha (2020) created a Maithili POS tagger using CRF and neural word embeddings, reaching 85.88% accuracy with a 52,190-word corpus, but the system’s performance may degrade for out-of-vocabulary or code-switched text. cite42†Mundotiya et al.\nL100: (2022) developed annotated corpora and POS/chunking tools for Bhojpuri, Maithili, and Magahi, showing high accuracy for SAHBiLC and FineSAHBiLC models (e.g., 0.86%–0.95% for POS and chunking); still, their approach is limited by the static nature of the corpus and its lack of domain diversity.\nL101: Note: Word vectorization techniques, in general, have proven to be transformational for low-resource languages, particularly since 2013, yet the challenge remains in crafting embeddings that reflect the linguistic and morphological intricacies of underrepresented languages without relying excessively on sister-language transfer models. While transfer learning and distributional models enable rapid development, they often inherit biases and linguistic mismatches from their source languages.\nL102: ## 4 Methodology\nL103: In the development of maiBERT, a dedicated Transformer-based language model for the Mithila language, we adopt the TensorFlow implementation of the BERT^{12}^{12} 12 cite74†https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/en/model˙doc/bert†huggingface.co architecture, pretrained using the MLM objective.\nL104: Given an input sequence $\\mathbf{x}=[x_{1},x_{2},\\ldots,x_{n}]$, the model first maps each token $x_{i}$ into a corresponding embedding ${e}_{i}\\in{R}^{d}$, where $d$ denotes the embedding dimension, forming an input matrix ${E}\\in{R}^{n\\times d}$. This matrix is combined with positional encodings ${P}\\in{R}^{n\\times d}$, ensuring sequential order preservation: ${Z}^{(0)}={E}+{P}$.\nL105: Each Transformer layer applies a multi-head self-attention mechanism, wherein attention heads $h$ compute the scaled dot-product attention function as shown in Equation (1).\nL106:  | $$\\text{Attention}({Q},{K},{V})=\\text{softmax}\\left(\\frac{{Q}{K}^{\\top}}{\\sqrt{d_{k}}}\\right){V}$$  |  | (1)\nL107: where query, key, and value matrices ${Q},{K},{V}\\in{R}^{n\\times d_{k}}$ are derived via learned linear projections. Multiple heads capture diverse syntactic and semantic patterns, concatenated and projected to form the output ${Z}^{(l+1)}$. The stacked encoder layers include residual connections and layer normalization, enabling gradient flow across layers and mitigating vanishing gradients.\nL108: The MLM training objective stochastically masks a subset of tokens $x_{i}$ in the input sequence and optimizes the Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL) of correctly predicting the masked tokens as shown in Equation (2).\nL109:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{MLM}}=-\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{M}}\\log P(x_{i}\\mid{x}_{\\mathcal{M}})$$  |  | (2)\nL110: where $\\mathcal{M}\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ denotes the masked positions and $\\mathbf{x}_{\\mathcal{M}}$ represents the input sequence with masked tokens. We train a Mithila language Language model with text corpus sourced from MithilaTextCorpus-v1 (refer to Section cite8†2.1 ), ensuring a representative distribution of regional grammar, morphology, and vocabulary richness.\nL111: The corpus was tokenized using SentencePiece-BPE, a subword-level tokenizer based on Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE), yielding a vocabulary $\\mathcal{V}$ of size 30,000, ensuring subword-level coverage of rare and compound words. Each training sample was tokenized into sequences of length $n\\leq 512$, padded or truncated appropriately. The final vocabulary embedding matrix ${W}_{e}\\in{R}^{|\\mathcal{V}|\\times d}={R}^{30000\\times 768}$ was learned jointly with model parameters.\nL112: Training was conducted over 1,000,000 steps with a batch size of 64 and a learning rate $\\eta=5\\times 10^{-5}$, using the Adam optimizer with bias correction and weight decay regularization. Table cite75†2 shows the training progress across key epochs, demonstrating rapid convergence of both loss and perplexity metrics. Gradient updates followed the update rule according to Equation (3):\nL113:  | $$\\theta_{t+1}\\leftarrow\\theta_{t}-\\eta\\cdot\\frac{\\hat{m}_{t}}{\\sqrt{\\hat{v}_{t}}+\\epsilon}$$  |  | (3)\nL114: where $\\hat{m}_{t}$ and $\\hat{v}_{t}$ are bias-corrected estimates of the first and second moments of gradients. The model was implemented using TensorFlow’s^{13}^{13} 13 TFAutoModelForMaskedLM API, allowing compatibility with Hugging Face’s pretrained model interface. During inference or downstream fine-tuning, the model can be easily reloaded using TensorFlow’s transformer ecosystem. For text generation or completion, masked token positions are inferred using Maximum a Posteriori (MAP) estimates.\nL115: For classification tasks, the output hidden state ${H}\\in{R}^{n\\times d}$ from the final encoder layer is pooled using the CLS token representation ${h}_{\\text{[CLS]}}$, then passed through a task-specific classification head ${y}=\\text{softmax}({W}_{c}{h}+{b}_{c})$ with cross-entropy loss as shown in Equation (4).\nL116:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{CE}}=-\\sum_{i=1}^{C}y_{i}\\log(\\hat{y}_{i})$$  |  | (4)\nL117: where $C$ is the number of classes. Fine-tuning for sequence classification tasks employs the TFAutoModelForSequenceClassification interface, supporting task adaptation on custom Mithila language datasets. The model was trained on NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPUs with 24 GB VRAM using mixed-precision training (FP16) to reduce memory usage and accelerate matrix operations.\nL118: Training leveraged TensorFlow’s XLA compiler and tf.keras.mixed_precision policy for automatic loss scaling and efficient GPU utilization, resulting in improved throughput and reduced training time without compromising model accuracy. To mitigate overfitting, dropout regularization $\\mathcal{D}(p=0.1)$, early stopping with a patience of 3 epochs based on validation loss, and weight decay penalties $\\lambda\\|\\theta\\|^{2}$ with $\\lambda=0.01$ were applied.\nL119: Token masking followed the BERT convention, with 15% of tokens masked: 80% replaced with [MASK], 10% replaced with a random token, and 10% left unchanged. maiBERT embedding space offers potential for visualization and semantic similarity tasks, whereby cosine similarity $\\cos(\\theta)=\\frac{{a}\\cdot{b}}{\\|{a}\\|\\|{b}\\|}$ is used to quantify proximity in the learned semantic space. Algorithm cite76†1 represents the entire flow of maiBERT.\nL120: Algorithm 1 : maiBERT: Pretraining and Fine-tuning Procedure\nL121: \nL122: 1 Corpus $\\mathcal{C}$, vocabulary size $|\\mathcal{V}|=30000$, sequence length $n\\leq 512$, embedding dimension $d=768$, learning rate $\\eta=5\\times 10^{-5}$, batch size = 64, total steps $T=1{,}000{,}000$\nL123: \nL124: 2 Pretrained Transformer-based masked language model for Mithila language\nL125: \nL126: 3 Tokenization:\nL127: \nL128: 4  Use SentencePiece-BPE to tokenize corpus $\\mathcal{C}$ into subword units\nL129: \nL130: 5  Build vocabulary $\\mathcal{V}$ of size 30,000\nL131: 6  Convert sentences to token sequences of max length $n$, pad/truncate as necessary\nL132: \nL133: 7 Embedding Initialization:\nL134: \nL135: 8  Initialize token embeddings $\\mathbf{E}\\in{R}^{n\\times d}$ and positional encodings $\\mathbf{P}\\in{R}^{n\\times d}$\nL136: \nL137: 9  Form input to model: $\\mathbf{Z}^{(0)}=\\mathbf{E}+\\mathbf{P}$\nL138: \nL139: 10 Training Loop:\nL140: \nL141: 11 for $t=1$ to $T$ do\nL142: \nL143: 12   Sample batch of tokenized sequences $\\mathbf{x}=[x_{1},x_{2},\\ldots,x_{n}]$\nL144: \nL145: 13   Randomly mask 15% of tokens to form $\\mathbf{x}_{\\mathcal{M}}$:\nL146: 14  80% replaced with [MASK], 10% with random token, 10% unchanged\nL147: \nL148: 15   Compute model output $\\hat{x}_{i}$ for masked positions using MLM\nL149: \nL150: 16   Compute MLM loss:\nL151:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{MLM}}=-\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{M}}\\log P(x_{i}\\mid\\mathbf{x}_{\\mathcal{M}})$$  |\nL152: \nL153: 17   Compute gradients and update parameters using Adam optimizer:\nL154:  | $$\\theta_{t+1}\\leftarrow\\theta_{t}-\\eta\\cdot\\frac{\\hat{m}_{t}}{\\sqrt{\\hat{v}_{t}}+\\epsilon}$$  |\nL155: 18   Apply regularization: weight decay $\\lambda\\|\\theta\\|^{2}$, dropout $\\mathcal{D}(p)$\nL156: \nL157: 19 end for\nL158: \nL159: 20 Fine-Tuning:\nL160: \nL161: 21 if Downstream task is classification then\nL162: \nL163: 22   Use output hidden states $\\mathbf{H}\\in{R}^{n\\times d}$\nL164: \nL165: 23   Extract [CLS] representation $h_{\\text{[CLS]}}$\nL166: \nL167: 24   Apply classification head:\nL168:  | $$\\hat{y}=\\text{softmax}(\\mathbf{W}_{c}h+\\mathbf{b}_{c})$$  |\nL169: \nL170: 25   Compute cross-entropy loss:\nL171:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{CE}}=-\\sum_{i=1}^{C}y_{i}\\log(\\hat{y}_{i})$$  |\nL172: 26   Train using TFAutoModelForSequenceClassification\nL173: \nL174: 27 end if\nL175: \nL176: 28 Inference:\nL177: \nL178: 29 Use MAP estimation for masked token prediction\nL179: \nL180: 30 Compute cosine similarity between embeddings for semantic tasks:\nL181:  | $$\\cos(\\theta)=\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}}{\\|\\mathbf{a}\\|\\|\\mathbf{b}\\|}$$  |\nL182: ## 5 Experimental Setup\nL183: \nL184: ### 5.1 Evaluation Metrics\nL185: \nL186: To rigorously assess the performance of maiBERT, we employ a comprehensive suite of evaluation metrics suited for multi-class and imbalanced classification tasks. These include accuracy (%), macro-precision (%), macro-recall (%), and macro-F1-score (%).\nL187: ### 5.2 Baselines\nL188: To assess the effectiveness of maiBERT, we compared its performance against a set of strong baseline models specifically designed for multilingual and low-resource Indic languages. These include NepBERTa (cite77†Timilsina et al., 2022 ), mBERT (cite78†Gonen et al., 2020 ), MuRIL (cite79†Khanuja et al., 2021 ), HindiBERT (cite29†Joshi, 2022 ), and NepaliBERT (cite30†Pudasaini et al., 2023 ).\nL189: NepBERTa (cite77†Timilsina et al., 2022 ) is a RoBERTa-based model pre-trained on approximately 0.8 billion words of Nepali text, tailored to capture the linguistic nuances of the Nepali language. mBERT (cite78†Gonen et al., 2020 ), trained on Wikipedia data from 104 languages, covers 3.3 billion tokens and serves as a widely used multilingual benchmark.\nL190: MuRIL (cite79†Khanuja et al., 2021 ) was pre-trained on a diverse mix of resources, including Wikipedia, translated corpora, OSCAR, and other Indian language datasets, comprising over 16 billion unique tokens. HindiBERT (cite29†Joshi, 2022 ) focuses on Hindi and Marathi, leveraging around 2.5 billion tokens from monolingual sources.\nL191: NepaliBERT (cite30†Pudasaini et al., 2023 ) was trained on curated Nepali datasets, including the NepaliBERT (cite30†Pudasaini et al., 2023 ) corpus and additional Nepali-specific resources, encompassing approximately 0.8 billion tokens.\nL192: Note: In the tables, Blue highlights the best performance, while Green indicates the second-best.\nL193: ## 6 Results and Discussion\nL194: ### 6.1 LLM Pre-training\nL195: \nL196: We trained MaiBERT in the masked language modeling (MLM) fashion on the Mithila corpus (Section cite8†2.1 ). Text was normalized (Devanagari-only, NFC), sentence-segmented, and tokenized with SentencePiece-BPE (|V| = 30,000). Inputs were truncated/padded to 512 tokens and masked following BERT’s convention (15% masked: 80% [MASK], 10% random, 10% unchanged). We note down Training Loss and Perplexity in Table cite75†2 .\nL197: Epoch  | Training Loss  | Perplexity\nL198: --- | --- | ---\nL199: 1  | 0.45  | 1.23\nL200: 3  | 0.37  | 1.01\nL201: 6  | 0.21  | 1.00\nL202: 10  | 0.012  | 1.00\nL203: Table 2: MLM pre-training progress showing convergence of loss and perplexity metrics across epochs.\nL204: Model  | Maithali Classification Task\nL205: --- | ---\nL206: Metric  | Acc  | Macro-Prec  | Macro-Rec  | Macro-F1\nL207: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL208: NepBERTa (cite77†Timilsina et al., 2022 )  | 86.4  | 85.7  | 85.9  | 86.0\nL209: mBERT (cite78†Gonen et al., 2020 )  | 55.2  | 54.5  | 53.8  | 54.1\nL210: MuRIL (cite79†Khanuja et al., 2021 )  | 86.3  | 85.9  | 86.1  | 86.0\nL211: HindiBERT (cite29†Joshi, 2022 )  | 86.89  | 86.2  | 86.4  | 86.3\nL212: NepaliBERT (cite30†Pudasaini et al., 2023 )  | 82.0  | 81.5  | 81.2  | 81.3\nL213: maiBERT (Ours)  | 87.02  | 86.8  | 87.0  | 86.9\nL214: Table 3: Performance Comparison of Baseline Models on Text Classification Task\nL215: Model  | Params  | Mem  | Time  | Ratio\nL216:  | (B)  | (GB)  | (hrs)  | (%)\nL217: maiBERT  | 0.11  | 4.6  | 0.85  | 172.11\nL218: Table 4: Computational Requirements of maiBERT. Ratio refers to Efficiency Ratio.\nL219: ### 6.2 Downstream Classification and Comparison with SOTA\nL220: Table cite80†3 demonstrates the performance of maiBERT. It handles Maithili news classification much better compared to existing state-of-the-art multilingual and Indic-specific baseline models. This consistent outperformance across all metrics can be attributed to several architectural and corpus-specific factors unique to maiBERT.\nL221: First, unlike generic multilingual models such as mBERT (cite78†Gonen et al., 2020 ) and MuRIL (cite79†Khanuja et al., 2021 ), which are trained across a wide range of languages with varying resource availability, maiBERT is explicitly fine-tuned on a richly preprocessed and linguistically diverse Maithili corpus.\nL222: This specialization enables it to better capture syntactic, semantic, and morphological nuances specific to the Maithili language, such as complex verb conjugations, dialectal variations, and usage of regional idioms, which are often underrepresented in large-scale multilingual pretraining.\n"
